\refcardt{Remark}{How DPO differs from reward-model PPO}{Sections 3--4}{rafailov2023direct}{
\p{DPO optimizes a policy directly on a fixed preference dataset. Reward-model
PPO instead fits an explicit scalar reward predictor, generates policy
rollouts, estimates advantages, and applies a policy-gradient update. DPO
therefore removes the separately represented reward and online PPO loop from
that training stage.}

\p{The methods still share ingredients: comparison data, a reference policy,
a KL coefficient or temperature, and assumptions connecting comparisons with
latent reward. Neither method defines preference optimization in general, and
their data and compute requirements are not causally interchangeable.}
}
